## 5. Controlled powering of the exact endpoint

The distinction between U0 and Uhat matters because n is large. From (5),
||Uhat||<=1+e0. Noncommutative telescoping against unitary U0 gives

    ||Uhat^n-U0^n||
       <=e0 sum_(j=0)^(n-1)(1+e0)^j
       <=n e0(1+e0)^(n-1)
       <=2n e0=:eta,                                               (6)

provided n e0<1/2. The code checks that condition for the actual integer.
For this n, eta=62208n/2^321; it is far smaller than the recurrence margin.
No claim that Uhat is unitary is used.

Binary powering starts with result I and base Uhat. For every bit, it
multiplies result by base if that bit is set and squares base for the next
bit. At the exact-matrix level, result and base are known powers of the
same Uhat; at the rectangle level, each multiplication encloses all allowed
products. Reusing the same endpoint rectangle can widen intervals because
correlations are ignored, but does not exclude the actual Uhat^n. All
rounding is directed. The actual computation used 187 bits and 274 interval
matrix multiplications. POWER_TRACE.json retains every step's result/base
rectangles, used bit and remaining exponent, not just a final scalar claim.

Let the computed residual rectangle enclose Uhat^n-I. For each entry, let
b_R and b_I be the maximum absolute integer real and imaginary endpoints.
Then the exact rational

    D^2=sum_entries (b_R^2+b_I^2)/S^2                              (7)

is a squared Frobenius upper bound and therefore a squared operator upper
bound. With positive m=r/4-eta, the decisive exact test is D^2<m^2. The
program also computes an integer ceiling of sqrt(sum(b_R^2+b_I^2)), giving
a separate explicit rational bound D_upper+eta<r/4. Both tests succeeded.
Finally the exact comparison against (10^-13-eta)^2 succeeded, yielding
(2). All fractions and every residual interval remain in the output.

