## 9. Failed shortcuts and honest boundary

The following routes were rejected analytically, not run as extra science:

- Powering the parent's original degree-80 enclosure with its old error
  allowance does not establish the required smallness after multiplication
  by this n. That is why the endpoint was recomputed at N320/S768.
- A small floating eigenphase residual alone is not a certificate for a
  large n. Phase error can be amplified by n, and scalar removal changes
  the comparator. Here direct interval powering replaces that inference.
- Normalizing to determinant one can give a false full-phase recurrence:
  for U=exp(i phi)I, such normalization removes the entire evolution while
  ||U^n-I||=2|sin(n phi/2)| need not be small. The actual source performs
  no such normalization.
- Naively treating Uhat as exactly unitary would omit (6). The code prices
  its norm growth and checks the actual amplification condition.
- Choosing a favorable row after a failed rigorous power test would be a
  second attempt. No such retry occurred; only one selected n was tested.

No universal obstruction is inferred from any rejected shortcut. The new
result closes only a finite mathematical witness-selection interface for
this exact nominal endpoint, subject to separate checking.

